id: rule-consequence-weaker
title: Weaker precondition
topic: Consequence
example-of: CONSEQUENCE
derived: false

task:
  Use the consequence rule to try to derive this triple. We take $Q'$
  equal to $Q$ and compute $P'$ with the rule ASSIGN.

pre: x > 90
post: x > 101

scheme: CONSEQUENCE
  First premise: «P» ==> «P'»
  Middle premise: {«P'»} x := x + 1 {«Q'»}
  Last premise: «Q'» ==> «Q»
  ---
  {«P»} x := x + 1 {«Q»}

program:
  {P}
  {P'}
  x := x + 1     // i1
  {Q'}
  {Q}

verdict: invalid

step: Q' and the substitution
part: Middle premise
rule: ASSIGN
focus: i1
text:
  We take $Q' = Q$. Then $P'$ is obtained by substituting the variable $x$
  with the assignment expression $x + 1$ in $Q'$.
set: Q' = x > 101
calc:
  wp(x := x + 1, Q')
  = (x > 101)[x <- x + 1]     // Q' is x > 101
  = x + 1 > 101               // x replaced by x + 1
set: P' = x + 1 > 101

step: Simplify P'
part: Middle premise
rule: LOGIC
focus: P'
text:
  We subtract 1 from both sides.
calc:
  x + 1 > 101
  = x > 100                   // subtract 1 from both sides
set: P' = x > 100

step: P does not imply P'
part: First premise
rule: CONSEQUENCE
focus: P, P'
text:
  >>> «P» ==> «P'»
vc: x > 90 ==> x > 100
status: fails
counterexample: x = 95
why:
  The state $x = 95$ satisfies $P$ and not $P'$. From this state the
  program gives $x = 96$, so $Q$ is false at the end. The triple is not
  valid.
